                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h1#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Adámek’s fixed point theorem
            gives a method to construct an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} initial algebra of an endofunctor
            by “iterating” the functor. The fact that this gives a “fixed point” of the endofunctor is given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambek%27s+theorem"} Lambek's theorem
          %h2#construction Construction
          %p
            This theorem is attributed to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"} Jiří Adámek
            but note that the same construction for set-functors already appeared in (
            %a{:href => "#Pohlova"}> Pohlova,1973
            )).
          #AdameksTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_admek Theorem (Adámek)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a category with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+composition"} transfinite composition
              of length
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              of sequences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \to C
              (where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              is the first infinite
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal"} ordinal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: C \to C
              preserves colimits of C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              \-chains. Then the colimit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
              of the chain
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \overset{i}{\to} F(0) \overset{F(i)}{\to} \ldots \to F^{(n)}(0) \overset{F^{(n)}(i)}{\to} F^{(n+1)}(0) \to \ldots
            %p
              carries the structure of an initial
              = succeed "-algebra." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mi γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\gamma \to \gamma
              is inverse to the canonical map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mi γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \to F\gamma
              out of the colimit (which is invertible by the hypothesis on
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} terminal coalgebra
          %p
            This approach can be generalized to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+construction+of+free+algebras"} transfinite construction of free algebras
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene%27s+fixed+point+theorem"} Kleene's fixed point theorem
                is precisely the
